{"person_id":"fe-010-11-radia-perlman","name":"Radia Perlman","cohort_group":"Researchers / independent engineers","category":"Network engineer and protocol designer","birth_year":1951,"age_at_milestone":24,"milestone_by_age_26":"Developed TORTIS (Toddler's Own Recursive Turtle Interpreter System) at MIT's AI Lab between 1974 and 1976, a system for teaching programming to children as young as three, which pioneered the field of tangible computing.","early_history_summary":"Perlman was a mathematically gifted student who entered MIT in the late 1960s as one of about 50 women in a class of 1,000. She began programming at the MIT AI Lab in 1971 as an undergraduate, working under Seymour Papert in the LOGO group. Inspired by LOGO, she designed TORTIS, hardware and software that allowed preschool-aged children to control a LOGO turtle robot, pioneering the field of tangible user interfaces.","primary_leverage_engine":"Technical depth","normalized_primary_engine":"Scarce technical / intellectual depth","secondary_engine":"Elite institutional network","started_serious_reps_before_20_score":1,"prior_reps_score":2,"scarce_skill_depth_score":2,"native_distribution_score":0,"elite_ecosystem_network_score":2,"complementary_team_score":0,"structural_wave_score":1,"concentration_intensity_score":1,"capital_safety_score":1,"domain_proximity_score":1,"total_leverage_score":11,"success_tier":1,"leverage_evidence_confidence":"Medium","family_context_summary":"Both parents were engineers for the US government; her father worked on radar and her mother was a mathematician by training who worked as a computer programmer.","parent_family_domain_summary":"Both parents had directly relevant technical expertise: father in radar engineering and mother in computer programming, providing early exposure to mathematical and computing concepts.","early_family_financial_platform_support_score":1,"parent_family_domain_advantage_score":2,"inherited_audience_business_network_score":0,"elite_institution_performance_pipeline_score":2,"frontier_geography_ecosystem_score":2,"rare_early_tools_facilities_score":2,"dedicated_mentor_coach_tutor_score":1,"exceptional_peer_cofounder_sibling_score":0,"early_online_platform_community_score":0,"direct_customer_domain_exposure_score":1,"prodigy_physical_edge_score":1,"adversity_constraint_catalyst_score":1,"inherited_access_stack_count":9,"exceptional_inherited_access_count":4,"all_documented_early_condition_count":9,"primary_early_advantage_archetype":"Institutional ecosystem acceleration","primary_early_advantage_tags":"MIT AI Lab, Seymour Papert, LOGO, family engineering background, early computing access, tangible computing","evidence_summary":"Radia Perlman was born to two engineer parents in Portsmouth, Virginia, and grew up in New Jersey where she was the top math and science student in her school. She entered MIT in the late 1960s as one of very few women and began paid programming work at the MIT AI Lab in 1971 at age 19. Under Seymour Papert's supervision in the LOGO group, she developed TORTIS (1974-76), a system enabling children as young as three to program a turtle robot, which later inspired the field of tangible user interfaces. Her parents' dual engineering background provided early domain exposure, while MIT's AI Lab gave her access to frontier computing facilities and mentorship that were extraordinarily rare for the era. She earned her SB (1973) and SM (1976) in mathematics from MIT before joining BBN Technologies to begin her networking career.","early_advantage_evidence_confidence":"High","source_count":5,"primary_source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radia_Perlman","source_urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radia_Perlman","https://www.invent.org/inductees/radia-perlman","https://jwa.org/encyclopedia/article/perlman-radia","https://www.technologyreview.com/2019/08/21/133561/radia-perlman-73-sm-76-phd-88/","http://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/5784"],"annotation_status":"subagent_researched_beta","source_audit_status":"not_independently_audited","data_version":"0.3.0-trajectory-beta","eligibility_status":"age_26_eligible","starting_point":"Born to a family of engineers in Portsmouth, Virginia; father worked on radar and mother was a mathematician and computer programmer for the US government. Grew up in Loch Arbour, New Jersey.","current_position":"Fellow at Dell Technologies as of 2022; holds over 100 patents, member of the National Academy of Engineering, inducted into both the Internet Hall of Fame (2014) and National Inventors Hall of Fame (2016).","current_position_year":2022,"is_living":true,"trajectory":[{"year":1971,"age":19,"event":"Began paid programming work at the MIT AI Lab in the LOGO group, writing system software such as debuggers."},{"year":1973,"age":21,"event":"Received SB in Mathematics from MIT."},{"year":1974,"age":22,"event":"Published TORTIS, a child-friendly programming system at MIT's AI Lab that pioneered tangible computing for preschool children."},{"year":1976,"age":24,"event":"Received SM in Mathematics from MIT and joined BBN Technologies to begin designing network protocols."},{"year":1984,"age":32,"event":"Invented the Spanning Tree Protocol at Digital Equipment Corporation, a fundamental algorithm for network bridging still used today."},{"year":1988,"age":36,"event":"Earned PhD in Computer Science from MIT with thesis on Byzantine-robust network layer protocols."},{"year":2014,"age":62,"event":"Inducted into the Internet Hall of Fame for contributions to Internet routing and bridging protocols."},{"year":2016,"age":64,"event":"Inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame for the Spanning Tree Protocol and other networking innovations."}]}
